sections.body:
In modern React, a **component** is, at its simplest, a JavaScript function that accepts a single argument, an object of **props**, and returns a description of what should appear on screen (a React element, typically written with JSX). A component is a function; calling it (which React does for you) produces a description of UI, not a direct DOM mutation.

```jsx
function Greeting(props) {
  return <h1>Hello, {props.name}!</h1>;
}
```

Components compose the way regular functions do: a component can render other components inside its returned JSX, so an application ends up as a tree of components calling other components, mirroring a call tree. One naming rule matters: component names must start with a capital letter (`Greeting`, not `greeting`). That is how JSX tells `<Greeting />` (a reference to your component function) from `<div>` or `<span>` (a built-in HTML tag).

JSX looks like HTML, which makes it easy to misread as HTML embedded inside JavaScript. It is not. JSX is a syntax extension that a build tool (typically Babel) compiles to ordinary JavaScript **before** it reaches the browser, into nested calls to `React.createElement(type, props, ...children)`. (Newer toolchains use the automatic JSX runtime, which emits equivalent `jsx()` calls from `react/jsx-runtime` and means you no longer need `import React`; the `createElement` form is the older, easier-to-read equivalent.)

```jsx
const element = <h1 className="title">Hello, {name}!</h1>;

// The line above compiles to something equivalent to:
const element = React.createElement(
  "h1",
  { className: "title" },
  "Hello, ",
  name,
  "!"
);
```

Because JSX is JavaScript function calls in disguise, several of its rules follow from that fact rather than being arbitrary. Attributes use `{ }` to embed any JavaScript *expression* (`className={isActive ? "active" : ""}`), not just string literals. Attribute names are camelCase (`className`, `onClick`) because they're JavaScript object property names passed to `createElement`, and `class` is a reserved word in JavaScript. Tags without children must be self-closed (`<img />`, not `<img>`), since JSX has no notion of tags that never have children, as HTML does. And a component's `return` can only produce a single root element (or a `Fragment`, `<>...</>`), because a JavaScript function returns one value and `React.createElement` calls nest like any other function call. Browsers don't understand JSX, so it always needs a compile step.

examples.code:
// IMPERATIVE: you manage every DOM update by hand
function renderImperativeCounter(container, count) {
  container.innerHTML = "";
  const button = document.createElement("button");
  button.textContent = `Clicked ${count} times`;
  button.onclick = () => renderImperativeCounter(container, count + 1);
  container.appendChild(button);
}

// DECLARATIVE: describe what the button should look like for a given count
// React handles finding and updating the real DOM node for you
function Counter({ count, onIncrement }) {
  return (
    <button onClick={onIncrement}>
      Clicked {count} times
    </button>
  );
}

const name = "Amara";
const element = <h1 className="title">Hello, {name}!</h1>;

// The JSX above is syntactic sugar for this ordinary JavaScript call:
const sameElement = React.createElement(
  "h1",
  { className: "title" },
  "Hello, ",
  name,
  "!"
);

function Greeting({ name, isNewUser }) {
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Hello, {name}!</h2>
      {isNewUser && <p>Welcome -- thanks for joining.</p>}
    </div>
  );
}

// Usage from a parent component:
<Greeting name="Amara" isNewUser={true} />
<Greeting name="Ben" isNewUser={false} />

